course: dictionary
lesson: 5942
title: A very serious word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words carry a lot of weight. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it for clues. «The Nanjing massacre of 1937–1938 is widely documented» tells you the word is about a historical event that people write about and study, so it is serious.
explain: The words «terrible» and «remember» give more clues. In «a terrible massacre», terrible tells you the event was awful. When people «remember the massacre», they keep the memory alive so the terrible event is not forgotten.
explain: Massacre also has an informal, everyday meaning in sport. «It was a massacre: ten–nil!» is an exaggeration, meaning one side won very easily. The word is so strong that using it for a game is a joke, not the same as a real massacre.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The war brought terrible cruel killings of many people» still makes sense, so massacre means the cruel killing of many people who cannot defend themselves.
fact: A massacre is the cruel killing of many people who cannot defend themselves.
fact: Massacre can also be a verb: «to massacre people» means to kill many of them in this way.
fact: In sport, «a massacre» is an informal way to describe a very heavy defeat.
fact: Historians use massacre for terrible events that people remember and mourn, such as the Nanjing massacre of 1937–1938.
fact: To massacre a song or a language, in informal speech, means to perform it very badly.
exercise: «The Nanjing massacre of 1937–1938 is widely documented.» What is a massacre? | A very heavy defeat in a football match | A long walk in the hills | A song sung very badly | *The cruel killing of many people who cannot defend themselves
exercise: «It was a massacre: ten–nil!» What does massacre mean here? | A tie at the end | A quiet, calm game with no score | *A very heavy defeat, said as an exaggeration | A cruel killing of people
exercise: Find and copy one word that shows the events were very serious. «The war brought terrible massacres of Jewish communities.» | *terrible | brought | communities | war
test: «To massacre people is to kill many of them in this way.» What does to massacre mean? | To make a loud noise | *To kill many people in a cruel way | To be very tired after a long day | To tidy up a room
test: Ben says, «Our choir massacred that song!» What does he mean? | They sang it beautifully and won a prize | They wrote a new song | *They performed the song very badly | They forgot the words but kept singing
exam: Why do historians handle the word massacre carefully, and why do people remember these terrible events? | Because it is a happy word, so it is used at parties | Because it only means a game was won | *Because the events were serious and people still mourn them, so honest history must name them clearly | Because it is a short word that is easy to spell
